# derive-pay-cadence-from-accrual-rates

When an HR policy states per-pay-period accrual rates AND annual totals but never names the pay cadence, divide annual total by per-period rate to derive the number of pay periods per year, then map to a named schedule.

## Procedures
- 1. Locate both the annual accrual total (e.g., 40 hours/year) and the per-pay-period rate (e.g., 1.54 hours/pay-period) for at least one accrual tier in the policy document.
- 2. Divide: annual_total ÷ per_period_rate = number of pay periods per year (e.g., 40 ÷ 1.54 ≈ 26).
- 3. Cross-check with at least one other tier to confirm consistency (all tiers must yield the same quotient).
- 4. Map the result to a named cadence: 52 = weekly, 26 = biweekly, 24 = semi-monthly, 12 = monthly.
- 5. State the derived cadence with the math as supporting evidence.

## Conventions
- Always verify the derivation across multiple tiers — if any tier produces a different quotient the document may contain an error.
- Round to the nearest whole number; policy documents occasionally use truncated decimal accrual rates.

